openstep-intel1000 — acknowledgements and third-party notices

This driver is licensed under the BSD 2-Clause License; see LICENSE.
This file records what was consulted while writing it, and what was not.

The driver is original work written for OPENSTEP 4.2. Intel's manual for
the controller family is its primary specification, and two existing
BSD-licensed drivers were read while writing it. **No source code from
either was copied into this work, and neither is redistributed here** —
the local copies used during development live in `ref/`, which is
excluded from both the repository and the distribution tarball. They are
acknowledged because reading them saved real time, and because several
decisions in this driver are cited to them in the source comments.


Intel — "PCI/PCI-X Family of Gigabit Ethernet Controllers Software
Developer's Manual" (317453, rev 4.0)

    The primary specification. Register offsets and layouts, the
    mandatory settling time after a global reset, the multicast hash,
    the CSA interrupt-mask ordering requirement for the 82547, and the
    statistics-register semantics all come from here.


FreeBSD — sys/dev/e1000
Copyright (c) 2001-2020, Intel Corporation. SPDX: BSD-3-Clause

    The only readily available implementation that covers the 82547.
    Consulted for which parts of the family need the PHY reset issued
    before the MAC and which cannot accept a memory-space global reset
    (e1000_82541.c gates both on mac.type), for the set of receive
    descriptor error bits that mean a frame is bad
    (E1000_RXD_ERR_FRAME_ERR_MASK), and for the multicast hash.

    Its register headers were used as a second opinion: every offset in
    this driver was written down only after FreeBSD's e1000_regs.h and
    the Intel manual agreed on it.


Minix 3 — minix/drivers/net/e1000
Distributed under BSD-3-Clause. These files note that parts of them
derive from DragonflyBSD/FreeBSD.

    A compact implementation, useful as a structural reference for how
    little is actually required to drive one of these parts. Its
    treatment of the transmit-queue-empty and transmit-descriptor-written
    interrupt causes as interchangeable is followed here. It does not
    support the 82547.


NeXT — SMC16 and AMDPCSCSIDriver DriverKit examples, and the NextDev
documentation for loadable kernel servers

    Taken from the OPENSTEP installation itself and used as
    documentation of the platform's own conventions: the driver bundle
    build chain, the load-command syntax, and the DMA and PCI
    configuration-space idioms. NeXT's material, likewise not
    redistributed here.


On GPL sources

    The project set out to treat BSD-licensed drivers as the
    implementation reference and GPL ones (Linux, U-Boot) as background
    reading only, never as something to copy from; the rule is recorded
    in PLAN.md and ref/README.md. In the event no GPL e1000 source was
    even available on the development machine, which carries Linux
    headers but no driver sources. Nothing in this work derives from
    them.


If you later copy code from FreeBSD or Minix into this tree, their
copyright notices and licence terms have to travel with it. Nothing here
relieves that.
